  "Q9": {
    "answer": "Six changes DHS made between the proposed rule (2025-16554, 90 FR 42070) and the final rule (2026-14439, 91 FR 44976) affecting F-1 students are: (1) Extenuating-circumstances exception for graduate-level F-1 transfers: the NPRM barred graduate students from transferring without exception, whereas the final rule permits SEVP to authorize a transfer exception for extenuating circumstances (e.g., school closure or prolonged inability to hold in-person classes due to a natural disaster)—touched in 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(ii)(A) and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(8)(i)(C)-(D) (91 FR 45125, 45127), described at 91 FR 45043 and 45097. (2) Prospective-only application of the same/lower educational-level ban: the final rule added 'after September 15, 2026' so programs completed prior to the rule's effective date do not count—touched in 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(ii)(C) (91 FR 45125), described at 91 FR 45038, 45097, and 45104 n.321. (3) Two-year limit and Federal Register notice for delaying/suspending educational-objective rules: the NPRM included an indefinite suspension power, which the final rule limited to two years from the effective date (until September 14, 2028) and required Federal Register notice in addition to SEVP website notice—touched in 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(ii)(E) (91 FR 45125), described at 91 FR 44979, 45043-45044, and 45097. (4) Aligning the post-completion OPT requested start-date limit from 60 to 30 days: DHS overlooked this conforming edit in the NPRM and updated 8 CFR 214.2(f)(11)(i)(D) from '60' to '30' in the final rule—touched in 8 CFR 214.2(f)(11)(i)(D) (91 FR 45124), described at 91 FR 45097 and 45105. (5) Early program completion and failure-to-maintain-status departure rules: the final rule added explicit language requiring F-1 students (and dependents) who end study or training early to depart or take action to maintain lawful status within 30 days of the new end date, and requiring students who fail to maintain a full course of study or status to leave immediately—touched in 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(v) (91 FR 45125), described at 91 FR 44979, 45097, and 45105. (6) Clarifying public high school includes taxpayer-funded charter schools: the final rule added that a public high school (12-month aggregate cap) may include a charter school or similar school funded by U.S. taxpayers—touched in 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i)(C) (91 FR 45124), described at 91 FR 45096 and 45102. (Additional changes include clarifying in 8 CFR 214.2(f)(10)(ii)(D) that post-completion OPT students may seek admission through CBP after travel abroad or an EOS with USCIS, 91 FR 45097; changing 'resume classes' to 'begin or resume classes' in 8 CFR 214.2(f)(8)(i)(H), 91 FR 45097; and moving DHS's 6-month increment extension authority for the OPT I-539 exemption into standalone 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1)(iii), 91 FR 45097, 45101.)",
    "basis": "Final rule 2026-14439 (91 FR 44976-45131): Section II.C (91 FR 44978-44979), Section IV.J (91 FR 45038-45044), Section V.A (91 FR 45096-45097), Section V.E-F (91 FR 45101-45105), and final regulatory text at 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1)(iii) and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5), (f)(8), (f)(10), (f)(11) (91 FR 45123-45127); compared with proposed rule 2025-16554 (90 FR 42070).",
    "uncertainty": "None."
