  "Q11": {
    "answer": "Costs: The final regulatory text (8 CFR 214.1(c)(2), 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(A), (iv)) does not codify a specific dollar fee; it requires remitting 'the required fees' / 'the appropriate fee' specified in USCIS form instructions (generally Form I-539) plus any biometrics required under 8 CFR 103.16. In the preamble (91 FR 45110), DHS specifies that if an F-2 dependent files a separate Form I-539, a separate filing fee is required, whereas if the dependent files Form I-539A as part of the principal's Form I-539, only one fee is required. The final rule does not print a DHS fee-schedule dollar figure for Form I-539 in the FR text (referring instead to the standalone Regulatory Impact Analysis in the docket at regulations.gov, 91 FR 45115-45116), though a commenter cited in the preamble (91 FR 45055) refers to 'a $420 fee, potential biometrics,' and another commenter (91 FR 45056) cites 'over $800 due to additional filing fees, biometrics, and processing charges' for an au pair EOS. In the PRA/RIA sections (91 FR 45116, 45120), DHS estimates form time burdens (1.667 hours paper I-539; 1.0 hour e-file I-539; 0.35 hours I-539A; 1.17 hours biometrics), notes some applicants incur legal/assistance costs, and estimates $317.3-$317.9 million in annualized EOS costs across nonimmigrants (separately, $233 at 91 FR 45092, 45118 is DHS's estimated DSO/school cost per EOS request). Processing time: In the preamble (91 FR 45084), DHS reports that '80 percent of Form I-539 adjudications for F, M, and J aliens are completed in three months' (citing USCIS Case Processing Times as of Feb. 23, 2026, n.277), and notes EAD processing averages 60-120 days for foreign students (91 FR 45032, 45107). However, DHS repeatedly cautions that it 'cannot guarantee processing times for applications filed at USCIS due to numerous governing factors' (91 FR 45025, 45029) and acknowledges backlogs and that the rule 'may cause an increase in USCIS processing times' (91 FR 45009, 45021, 45084; commenters cited ~3.5 months at 91 FR 44999, >6.5 months at 91 FR 45063, and >240 days / up to 18 months historically at 91 FR 45031). Draft handout sentences: 'To extend your stay, you must submit Form I-539 with the required USCIS filing fee (see USCIS's current fee schedule; only one filing fee is required if eligible F-2 dependents are included on the same application via Form I-539A) and complete any required biometrics. While USCIS recently completed 80% of F, M, and J Form I-539 applications in about three months, DHS cannot guarantee processing times and warns they may increase, so USCIS recommends filing up to six months before your authorized stay expires.'",
    "basis": "Final rule 2026-14439 (91 FR 44976): 8 CFR 214.1(c)(2) (91 FR 45122-45123), 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(A), (iv) (91 FR 45126); preamble discussions at 91 FR 44999, 45009, 45021, 45025, 45029, 45031-45032, 45055-45056, 45063, 45084, 45092, 45107, 45110, 45115-45116, 45118, 45120.",
    "uncertainty": "The exact USCIS Form I-539 paper/online/biometrics dollar fee table appears in the standalone RIA posted in the docket (regulations.gov) rather than in the Federal Register text of 2026-14439; the $420 figure appears only in a summary of a public comment (91 FR 45055), not as an agency statement in the rule."
  },
  "Q12": {
    "answer": "(a) Yes, it is timely, subject to a drafting discrepancy to note. Under 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1) (91 FR 45123), Priya's authorized period of stay runs until May 15, 2027 plus her 60-day transition departure period—July 14, 2027. Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(B) (91 FR 45126), an EOS application is timely filed if USCIS's receipt date is 'on or before the date the authorized period of stay expires' (and the final sentence reiterates that USCIS must receive it 'on or before the expiration of the previously authorized period of stay'). Preamble 91 FR 45102 likewise confirms D/S-transition students must file an EOS 'by their program end date... plus an additional 60 days for F nonimmigrants.' Note, however, a textual gap: 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(B) refers specifically to 'the 30-day period provided in paragraph (f)(5)(v)' rather than cross-referencing 214.1(m)(1)'s 60-day transition departure period. (b) No, she does NOT keep an automatic extension of on-campus or CPT employment while it is pending. First, under 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1) (91 FR 45123), her authorized employment or training ends on the Form I-20 program end date (May 15, 2027). Second, under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(viii) and (f)(7)(iii)(B) (91 FR 45125-45126), an F-1 student who files an EOS during the post-completion departure period receives NO automatic extension of authorized employment (including on-campus, CPT, and severe economic hardship) and must wait for USCIS approval of the EOS before engaging in employment or CPT. Recommendation to adviser: Advise Priya to obtain her updated Form I-20 and file Form I-539 before her I-20 program end date (May 15, 2027)—and as early as up to 6 months ahead as DHS recommends (91 FR 45107, 45110)—so she avoids any gap in work eligibility, avoids any risk from the 30-day vs. 60-day cross-reference gap in 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(B), and ensures the DSO enters any program extension in SEVIS before May 15, 2027 as required by 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(viii).",
    "basis": "Final regulatory text in 2026-14439 (91 FR 44976): 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1) (91 FR 45123); 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(viii), (f)(7)(iii)(B), (f)(7)(viii) (91 FR 45125-45127). Preamble at 91 FR 45102, 45107, 45109-45110.",
    "uncertainty": "8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(B) and (f)(5)(viii) explicitly cross-reference only the 30-day departure period in 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(v) rather than the 60-day transition departure period in 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1), though both 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(B)'s general 'expiration of the authorized period of stay' rule and preamble 91 FR 45102 support timeliness during the 60-day transition window."
  },
  "Q13": {
    "answer": "(a) June 13, 2027. When Sofia departed on December 19, 2026 and was readmitted on January 9, 2027 (after September 15, 2026), she exited the D/S transition group and was admitted for a fixed period under 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(i)(A), 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i), and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(v) (see also preamble 91 FR 45099, 45101). That fixed admission runs to her Form I-20 program end date (May 14, 2027) plus a 30-day departure period (not 60 days). Date arithmetic: May 14, 2027 + 30 days = 17 remaining days in May (May 15-31) + 13 days in June (June 1-13) = June 13, 2027. (b) Yes, she MUST file Form I-539. Under 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1)(ii) (and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(10)(ii)(D)), an F-1 transition student who departs the U.S. before filing for post-completion OPT and is readmitted with a fixed period of admission must file BOTH Form I-765 and Form I-539 (so the March 18, 2027 I-539 exemption in 214.1(m)(1)(i) does not apply to her). Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(B), USCIS must receive Form I-539 on or before her authorized period of stay expires, including the 30-day departure period—i.e., on or before June 13, 2027. (c) July 9, 2028. If her I-765 and I-539 are approved as requested (OPT through June 9, 2028), under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(v), (f)(7)(vi), and (f)(10)(ii)(D), her approved EOS grants the OPT end date plus a 30-day departure period. Date arithmetic: June 9, 2028 + 30 days = 21 remaining days in June (June 10-30) + 9 days in July (July 1-9) = July 9, 2028. (d) Under the alternative facts (no December trip; I-765 filed Feb. 22, 2027; trip May 20 to June 5, 2027 while I-765 is pending): (1) No, she does not need a Form I-539. She qualified for the 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1)(i) exemption when she filed Form I-765 on Feb. 22, 2027 (on or before March 18, 2027) without having departed prior to filing; under 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1)(ii), departing while Form I-765 is pending causes her to be admitted upon return under 8 CFR 214.1(a) (and under 8 CFR 214.1(c)(8)(ii) and 214.2(f)(10)(ii)(D), her pending I-765 is not abandoned and she may obtain her OPT stay via CBP admission). (2) On June 5, 2027, CBP may admit her under 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(iii) until the DSO's recommended OPT end date on Form I-20 (June 9, 2028) plus a 30-day period under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(v)—i.e., until July 9, 2028 (June 9, 2028 + 30 days = July 9, 2028). (3) Under 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(iii) and 8 CFR 214.1(c)(8)(iii) (and preamble 91 FR 45098, 45100 n.303), she should carry her Form I-797C receipt notice confirming USCIS receipt of Form I-765 and her latest Form I-20 endorsed by her DSO recommending post-completion OPT with the proposed OPT end date (along with valid passport/visa and arrangements to handle any RFE or EAD mailed to her U.S. address).",
    "basis": "Final regulatory text in 2026-14439 (91 FR 44976): 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(i)(A), (a)(4)(iii), (c)(8)(ii)-(iii), (m)(1)(i)-(ii) (91 FR 45122-45123); 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i), (f)(5)(v), (f)(7)(iii)(B), (f)(7)(vi), (f)(10)(ii)(D) (91 FR 45124-45127). Preamble at 91 FR 45098-45101.",
    "uncertainty": "Under 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(iii), CBP 'may' admit an F-1 student with a pending OPT I-765 until the DSO-recommended OPT end date plus 30 days, reflecting CBP discretion at the port of entry (preamble 91 FR 45100 n.303 also cautions that if the OPT is approved while abroad, she is expected to have the EAD in hand to re-enter)."
  },
  "Q14": {
    "answer": "(a) FR Doc. 2026-20660, cited as 91 FR 64566 (pages 64566-64600), published October 8, 2026; type: Proposed Rule (Notice of Proposed Rulemaking / NPRM); agency: Department of Homeland Security, U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE); docket number: DHS Docket No. ICEB-2026-0100; RIN: 1653-AB01. Current status: proposed rule only, NOT final and NOT in force. Deadlines: comments on the proposed rule were due on or before November 9, 2026 (closed as of Nov. 14, 2026); comments on the Paperwork Reduction Act information collection are due by December 7, 2026 (91 FR 64566). (b) $70,000 (not $30,000). Under proposed 8 CFR 214.13(i)(1)-(3) (91 FR 64600), the $30,000 subsequent OPT fee applies ONLY 'if the alien has previously participated in OPT and paid the initial OPT fee of $70,000.' Proposed 8 CFR 214.13(i)(3) ('Transition period') states explicitly that schools recommending 'any type of OPT' must pay $70,000 for each student who receives a DSO OPT recommendation dated on or after 60 days after publication of the final rule, and only subsequent recommendations for students 'for which the $70,000 OPT fee has been paid' are subject to the $30,000 fee. Because Daniel's 2026 post-completion OPT did not involve the $70,000 fee, his April 2027 STEM OPT recommendation would trigger the $70,000 fee. (c) Who pays: the SEVP-certified educational institution (proposed 8 CFR 214.13(i)(1)-(3), 214.2(f)(11)(ii)). When: BEFORE the DSO issues the OPT recommendation in SEVIS / on Form I-20 and before the student can apply to USCIS (proposed 8 CFR 214.2(f)(11)(ii)(B)-(C), 214.13(i)(1)-(2)). If not paid: the DSO cannot issue the recommendation, and under proposed 8 CFR 214.2(f)(11)(iii) (91 FR 64599), USCIS 'may not grant employment authorization to any student where the DSO issued the recommendation prior to the school paying the required OPT fees or where the school has failed to pay all applicable OPT fees.' Refundability: under proposed 8 CFR 214.13(i)(1)-(2) (91 FR 64600), SEVP has case-by-case discretion to grant a school's refund request (after the DSO removes the OPT recommendation in SEVIS, with no administrative appeal) ONLY if the student has NOT been issued the OPT EAD or granted the OPT extension; once the EAD/extension is issued, the fee 'will not be refunded under any circumstances.' (d) Under proposed 8 CFR 214.13(i)(3), the fee applies to any DSO OPT recommendation dated on or after 60 days after the publication date of the final rule. Thus, a DSO recommendation dated BEFORE the 60th day after final rule publication would fall outside the fee (since no final rule has been published as of Nov. 14, 2026, that 60-day cutoff could not arrive before mid-January 2027 at the earliest, though STEM OPT timing rules limit how early before EAD expiration a DSO can recommend and a student can file). (e) Under the rules in force today (2026-14439): because Daniel files on April 12, 2027 (after March 18, 2027), he is NOT exempt under 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1)(i) (unless DHS extends the date under 214.1(m)(1)(iii)), so under 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1) and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iii), (v) (preamble 91 FR 45034, 45100), he must file BOTH Form I-765 (with STEM OPT-endorsed Form I-20) and Form I-539 (EOS, with updated I-20, proof of funds, fee, and any required biometrics). Because he timely files his STEM OPT Form I-765 before his current EAD expires on June 30, 2027, under 8 CFR 274a.12(b)(6)(iv) (confirmed in 2026-14439 preamble at 91 FR 45024, 45037, 45101), his post-completion OPT work authorization is automatically extended for up to 180 days past June 30, 2027—date arithmetic: July (31) + Aug (31, total 62) + Sept (30, total 92) + Oct (31, total 123) + Nov (30, total 153) + Dec 1-27 (27, total 180) = December 27, 2027 (or until USCIS decides the I-765, if earlier). If both are approved for 24 months (July 1, 2027 through June 30, 2029), under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(vi) and (f)(5)(v) he may stay until June 30, 2029 + 30 days = July 30, 2029.",
    "basis": "Proposed rule 2026-20660 (91 FR 64566-64600, Oct. 8, 2026): DATES (91 FR 64566), proposed 8 CFR 214.2(f)(11)(i)-(iii) and 8 CFR 214.13(i)(1)-(3) (91 FR 64599-64600). Final rule 2026-14439 (91 FR 44976): 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1), (m)(1)(i), (m)(1)(iii) (91 FR 45123); 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(v), (f)(7)(iii), (f)(7)(v)-(vi) (91 FR 45125-45126); preamble on 8 CFR 274a.12(b)(6)(iv) at 91 FR 45002, 45024, 45037, 45101.",
    "uncertainty": "Whether or when DHS will finalize 2026-20660 (or keep the 60-day post-publication effective trigger) and whether DHS will extend the March 18, 2027 Form I-539 exemption under 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1)(iii) are unknown. Also, the exact pre-existing 90-day/60-day STEM OPT recommendation window under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(11)(i)(C) was not printed in full in 2026-14439."
  },
  "Q15": {
    "answer": "1. INCORRECT. Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(B) (91 FR 45126), an EOS is timely if USCIS receives it on or before the expiration of the authorized period of stay, which includes the 30-day departure period under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(v) (or 60-day transition period under 214.1(m)(1)); however, filing before the I-20 program end date is required to retain automatic on-campus/CPT work authorization (8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(viii), (f)(7)(iii)(B)), and the DSO must enter the extension in SEVIS before the I-20 end date (8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(viii)). Rewording: 'USCIS must receive an extension-of-stay application on or before the Form I-94 expiration date (which includes the 30-day departure period), though filing before the I-20 program end date is required to continue on-campus or CPT employment while the application is pending, and the DSO must update SEVIS before the I-20 end date.' 2. INCORRECT. Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i) (91 FR 45124), the up-to-30-day pre-program arrival period and 30-day departure period do not count toward the 4-year maximum; preamble Section V.B (91 FR 45098) explicitly states the 4-year period 'will not begin from the date of admission during that 30-day arrival window, but from the program start date.' Rewording: 'The four-year maximum period of admission is counted from the program start date (or report date) on Form I-20, not from the date of entry during the 30-day early-arrival window.' 3. CORRECT, with an internal regulatory-text tension to flag. Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(viii) (91 FR 45125) and preamble (91 FR 45043, 45049, 45106), a student with a timely filed EOS (received before the 30-day departure period) remains in authorized stay and may keep studying until USCIS decides, while on-campus employment is auto-extended for up to 240 days beginning from the I-94 admission end date, after which the student must stop working. Tension: within 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(viii) (91 FR 45125-45126), the substantive sentence starts the 240 days from the Form I-94 end date (matching preamble 91 FR 45049, 45106), whereas the Form I-9 sentence at the end of the same paragraph says the authorized employment period 'which ends 30 days before their Form I-94... admit until date' is considered unexpired 'for up to 240 days' (which could imply counting from 30 days before the I-94 date). The substantive grant in the regulatory text (240 days from the I-94 date) controls over preamble commentary, though advisers should note the Form I-9 phrasing and that filing during the 30-day departure period yields zero automatic work extension. 4. INCORRECT. Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i)(A) (91 FR 45124), standalone English language training students are capped at an aggregate 24-month admission period (plus 30 days departure), and preamble 91 FR 45039 explicitly states: 'Students who encounter extenuating circumstances such as illness, disability, or other emergencies during their studies, will still be limited to the 24-month admission period.' Rewording: 'A student in a standalone English language training program is limited to an aggregate maximum of 24 months (plus 30 days for departure) and cannot extend beyond 24 months even for illness or other emergencies.' 5. INCORRECT. Preamble 91 FR 45039-45040 expressly rejects this: 'That is untrue. If the program has been accredited as a 5-year program, the I-20 or DS-2019 will state the full program length. The student will need to apply for an EOS with USCIS prior to the expiration of his or her currently authorized period of stay using the same I-20 or DS-2019 used to enter the country' (consistent with 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i) and (f)(7)(i)(C)(1)). Rewording: 'A five-year accredited degree program does not need to be shortened or split across two I-20s; the I-20 shows the full five-year length, CBP admits the student for up to four years (plus 30 days), and the student files an extension of stay with USCIS using that I-20 to finish the fifth year.' 6. PARTLY CORRECT. Under 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(i)(A)-(B) (91 FR 45122) and preamble 91 FR 45098, a student in valid status who departs and is readmitted on a valid Form I-20 with a later program end date may be admitted by CBP for up to that new program end date (capped at 4 years, plus 30 days), making an EOS unnecessary for the newly admitted period (and abandoning a pending EOS under 214.1(c)(8)(ii)). However, CBP admission is discretionary ('may be admitted'), still capped at 4 years, cannot cure prior status violations or bypass 214.2(f)(5)(ii)(C)/(f)(8)(i)(C) restrictions, and does not avoid an EOS if the student instead asks to re-enter for the unexpired balance of a prior stay under 214.1(c)(8)(i). Rewording: 'A student in valid status who travels abroad and is readmitted by CBP on a valid I-20 with a later program end date may, in CBP's discretion, receive a new fixed admission period up to that end date (maximum 4 years plus 30 days), eliminating the need for a USCIS extension of stay for that admitted period (and abandoning any pending EOS).' 7. INCORRECT. Here the regulatory text and preamble diverge: several preamble passages (91 FR 44978, 45033, 45034) loosely describe the OPT Form I-539 exemption as applying 'within six months of the effective date' (which would suggest March 15, 2027), whereas the binding regulatory text at 8 CFR 214.1(m)(1)(i) (91 FR 45123)—and other preamble passages (91 FR 45017, 45031, 45033, 45034, 45101)—expressly specify the cutoff date as 'on or before March 18, 2027.' The regulatory text controls over loose preamble shorthand, so the last filing day is March 18, 2027 (and the exemption applies only to D/S-transition students who have not departed prior to filing under 214.1(m)(1)(ii)). Rewording: 'The temporary exemption from filing Form I-539 for OPT applicants covers timely Form I-765s filed on or before March 18, 2027 by D/S-transition F-1 students who have not departed the U.S. since September 15, 2026.'",
