    "answer": "(a) September 22, 2031. Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i) (91 FR 45124) and preamble 91 FR 45098, Lin's initial admission (she entered Aug. 2, 2027, 21 days before her Aug. 23, 2027 start date, within the 30-day early-arrival window) is capped at 4 years counted from her program start date (August 23, 2027)—NOT her entry date—plus a 30-day departure period under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i) and (v). Date arithmetic: August 23, 2027 + 4 years = August 23, 2031; August 23, 2031 + 30 days = 8 remaining days in August (Aug 24-31) + 22 days in September (Sept 1-22) = September 22, 2031. (b) To stay beyond September 22, 2031, Lin must apply to USCIS for an Extension of Stay (EOS) on Form I-539 (or successor designated form) on or before September 22, 2031 (or before August 23, 2031 if she needs uninterrupted on-campus/CPT work authorization under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(viii)). She submits: Form I-539, her updated and properly endorsed Form I-20 (reflecting additional time remaining in the PhD), evidence of sufficient funds to cover expenses, required filing fee, and any biometrics required under 8 CFR 103.16 (8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(i)-(iii)). Grounds: (1) 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(i)(C)(1) ('a currently issued Form I-20... indicating additional time is left to complete his or her program of study'; see preamble 91 FR 45039-45040) and/or (2) 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(i)(C)(2)(i) (preamble 91 FR 45108-45109 states students making normal progress whose program standard timeline exceeds 4 years qualify under compelling academic reasons). How early: preamble footnotes 197, 328, and 338 (91 FR 45040, 45107, 45110) state there is 'no prohibition on filing the form before a certain date ahead of expiration,' while DHS generally recommends filing within the 6 months (180 days) before expiration. If granted in full (through May 14, 2033, under 4 years from Aug. 23, 2031), under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(vi) and (f)(5)(v) she may stay until June 13, 2033 (May 14, 2033 + 30 days = 17 remaining days in May + 13 days in June = June 13, 2033). (c) No. Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(ii)(C), the bar applies after completing a program and seeking F-1 status for another program at the same or lower level; preamble 91 FR 45042-45043 explicitly states: 'a student issued a Form I-20 for a Ph.D. program as their primary academic objective could obtain a master's degree incidental to status while progressing toward completion of the Ph.D. program.' (d) Under 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(i)(A) and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i) (and preamble 91 FR 45040, 45098), when Lin seeks readmission in August 2030 with her valid Form I-20 ending May 14, 2033 (under 4 years away), CBP may admit her up to May 14, 2033 plus 30 days = June 13, 2033; if CBP does so, she will no longer need the EOS filing in (b). Certainty: Moderate/qualified—8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(i)(A) uses discretionary language ('may be admitted') and refers to admission 'after a previously authorized period of admission... expired' (whereas Lin's 2031 stay has not yet expired in 2030), though preamble 91 FR 45040 and 45098 confirm CBP may grant up to 4 years from the I-20 upon re-entry; Lin must inspect her new Form I-94 upon return to verify the admit-until date CBP actually issued. (e) Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i)(E) (91 FR 45124), '[t]he authorized period of stay for F-2 dependents may not exceed the authorized period of stay of the principal F-1 student.' Thus, despite the later date on his Form I-94, his authorized stay cannot exceed Lin's authorized stay (September 22, 2031, or June 13, 2033 if Lin's stay was already extended to June 13, 2033). When Lin applies under (b), under 8 CFR 214.1(c)(2) and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iv) (and preamble 91 FR 45110), her husband may either be included on Lin's Form I-539 (via Form I-539A) or file his own separate Form I-539, showing the qualifying marital relationship, maintenance of status, no unauthorized employment, sufficient funds, and biometrics. On the fee, preamble 91 FR 45110 states: 'If the dependent files a separate Form I-539, he or she will need to pay a separate Form I-539 filing fee. However, if the dependent files a Form I-539A as part of the primary applicant's EOS request on a Form I-539, only one fee will be required.'",
